A module of optional values, result values, panic helpers, and byte utilities.

A public record Option of T has a has_value flag as a truth value, and a value as T.

A public record Result of T and E has an is_ok flag as a truth value, an ok_val as T, and an err_val as E.

Publicly, to wrap_option for T given value as T, named option_some, returning Option of T.
value_out is Option of T.
Set value_out's has_value to true.
Set value_out's value to value.
Give back value_out.

Publicly, to empty_option for T, named option_none, returning Option of T.
value_out is Option of T.
Give back value_out.

Publicly, to check_option for T given option_value as a pointer to Option of T, named option_is_some, returning a truth value.
Give back option_value's has_value.

Publicly, to unwrap_option for T given option_value as a pointer to Option of T, fallback as T, named option_unwrap_or, returning T.
Give back option_value's value if option_value's has_value equals true, otherwise give back fallback.

Publicly, to succeed_result for T and E given value as T, named result_ok, returning Result of T and E.
value_out is Result of T and E.
Set value_out's is_ok to true.
Set value_out's ok_val to value.
Give back value_out.

Publicly, to fail_result for T and E given error_value as E, named result_err, returning Result of T and E.
value_out is Result of T and E.
Set value_out's is_ok to false.
Set value_out's err_val to error_value.
Give back value_out.

Publicly, to check_result for T and E given result_value as a pointer to Result of T and E, named result_is_ok, returning a truth value.
Give back result_value's is_ok.

Publicly, to unwrap_result for T and E given result_value as a pointer to Result of T and E, fallback as T, named result_unwrap_or, returning T.
Give back result_value's ok_val if result_value's is_ok equals true, otherwise give back fallback.

Publicly, to stop given msg as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, named panic, returning nothing.
Call this as "stop with message <msg> of length <len>".
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 2, msg, and len.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 2, "\n", and 1.
Ask Linux kernel 60 with 1.

Publicly, to check stop condition given cond as a truth value, msg as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, named assert_true, returning nothing.
If not cond, stop with message msg of length len.

Publicly, to clear given dst as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, named mem_zero, returning nothing.
Call this as "clear <len> bytes at <dst>".
Let i be 0 as an unsigned number.
While i is less than len, set item i of dst to 0, add 1 to i.

Publicly, to check_bytes given a as a pointer to byte, b as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, named mem_eq, returning a truth value.
Call this as "bytes at <a> match bytes at <b> for <len>".
Let i be 0 as an unsigned number.
While i is less than len, set i to i plus 1, if a[i minus 1] is not equal to b[i minus 1], give back false.
Give back true.
